  <h1>What is the Lantern Festival?</h1>
  <div class="zh">元宵节</div>
  <p>The first full-moon celebration of the traditional year: lantern light, riddles, round rice foods, and customs that vary across China.</p>
  <p style="color:#6a6153;font-size:13px;margin-top:10px">By <a href="/editorial-policy" style="color:inherit">yaopulife Editorial</a> · Published 2026-10-23 · Updated 2026-10-23</p>
</div></header>

<main class="wrap art">
<p style="color:#6a6153;font-size:13px;margin:18px 0"><a href="/">Home</a> · <a href="/festivals">Festivals</a> · Lantern Festival</p>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="answer">The quick answer</h2>
  <p class="lead" style="max-width:780px">The <b>Lantern Festival</b> (<span class="zh">元宵节</span> <i>Yuánxiāo Jié</i>) falls on the <b>fifteenth day of the first lunar month</b>, traditionally the first full-moon night of the new year. Lantern viewing, riddles, and round glutinous-rice foods are widely associated with the date, but the performances and food names differ by region.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="name">What “Yuanxiao” means</h2>
  <p>The name begins with the calendar. <i>Yuan</i> (<span class="zh">元</span>) refers here to the first lunar month, and <i>xiao</i> (<span class="zh">宵</span>) means night. The China Intangible Cultural Heritage project record explains that the fifteenth night of that month—the first full-moon night of the traditional year—is called Yuanxiao Festival.</p>
  <p>The same source records other names, including <b>Shangyuan Festival</b> (<span class="zh">上元节</span>) and <b>Lantern Festival</b> (<span class="zh">灯节</span>). These are overlapping historical names, not separate dates.</p>
  <div class="note"><span class="nlbl">Calendar note</span><b>The Gregorian date moves every year.</b> “First month” means the first month of the traditional Chinese calendar, not January. The festival therefore falls on a different Western-calendar date each year.</div>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="lanterns">Why lanterns and riddles are central</h2>
  <p>The national heritage record traces lantern lighting for Yuanxiao back to the Han period and describes the custom as increasingly prominent in later dynasties. The Palace Museum offers a concrete Qing-court example: its record for <i>Qianlong Emperor Enjoying the Lantern Festival</i> describes the emperor and imperial family celebrating, and its festival notes list lanterns, fireworks, riddles, and round rice foods.</p>
  <p>Lantern riddles (<span class="zh">猜灯谜</span> <i>cāi dēngmí</i>) turn a display into a public word game. The heritage record dates the established practice of hanging riddles for people to solve to the Song period. It also records later and regional additions such as dragon and lion dances, stilt walking, land-boat dances, and drums.</p>
  <p>These examples describe a living family of festival practices, not one required program. A neighborhood lantern display, a temple fair, and a museum event can all look different. Practice varies by region, community, and period.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="food">Yuanxiao, tangyuan, and the meaning of roundness</h2>
  <p>Round glutinous-rice foods are the festival's best-known dish. The heritage record connects eating yuanxiao with family unity and harmony; the Palace Museum likewise describes the food's association with reunion.</p>
  <p>The words <i>yuanxiao</i> (<span class="zh">元宵</span>) and <i>tangyuan</i> (<span class="zh">汤圆</span>) are sometimes used loosely, but a common modern distinction concerns how they are made. A Xinhua explainer says <b>yuanxiao are rolled in dry glutinous rice flour</b> around a prepared filling, while <b>tangyuan are wrapped in a moistened glutinous-rice dough</b>. The result is similar in outline—a filled, round rice food—but the method and texture differ.</p>
  <p>That distinction is useful, not absolute. Naming and practice vary by region, and institutional accounts sometimes use the terms together when describing the festival. It is safer to explain the preparation than to claim that one word is always “northern” and the other always “southern.”</p>
</section>

<section class="sec">
  <h2 id="history">What the historical sources do—and do not—prove</h2>
  <p>The cited records support a long history of lantern lighting, public celebration, riddles, and round rice foods. They also show change: customs expanded, and local performance traditions developed in different ways.</p>
  <p>They do not establish a single dramatic origin story for the whole festival. Popular retellings may connect Yuanxiao with an emperor, a celestial official, or a young woman whose name was Yuanxiao. Those stories are better presented as later legends unless a page identifies a particular text and version. This guide therefore keeps the documented calendar and customs separate from unverified single-start claims.</p>
</section>

